In February 2019, officers seized 240 overweight trucks and fined more than 600 million riels
(Phnom Penh): In February 2019, officials of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport led by Mr. Seng Chhuon, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Chairman of the Working Group to inspect 240 overweight vehicles with a total fine of 659 , 350,000 Riels. This is according to the report of the Ministry of Public Works, which was received by Fresh News on the afternoon of March 9, 2019.
A report by the Ministry of Public Works and Transport claims that the number of overweight trucks seized increased by 25 units from 215 in January 2019. According to the report, the seized vehicles were overweight from 5 tons to 57 tons.
With these hot operations to crack down on overweight trucks, Mr. Seng Chhuon stated that in accordance with the instructions of Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, the overweight vehicle inspection team will inspect the vehicles. Transport everywhere to prevent overloading that can cause serious damage to the road.
Mr. Seng Chhuon stressed that the operation to suppress the overweight truck will be carried out in a transparent manner without any intervention, even if the overweight truck belongs to an influential or powerful person.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol has confirmed that the operation to prevent overweight trucks is not slowing down and will continue on a regular basis. The senior minister further stated that the operation to prevent overweight trucks is done on all vehicles and equally without any intervention, even if the owners of those trucks are powerful, influential or have money.
The senior minister also called on truck owners to respect the transport laws and weight limits set by law to avoid damaging the roads that the government is trying to build. The senior minister said that the Royal Government as well as the Ministry did not want the fine for overweight because the money from the fine could not be offset by the amount of road damage and could not be used to repair the road. No.
