On March 8, 1899, textile and garment workers in Chicago and New York, United States, united in a united struggle for shorter hours. Work and raise salaries. But as a result, the workers were fully oppressed, arrested, tortured and expelled from the factory.
Despite the severe pain and danger, the workers remained united, fought hard, and repeatedly protested in public, forcing the factory owners to agree. The struggle of these workers has strongly encouraged the global women's movement.
In 1910, the 17th International Women's Conference was held in Copenhagen, the capital of Denmark, to mark March 8, "International Women's Day," a day of great solidarity for women workers around the world for the rights and freedoms of democracy. The slogan is "Work eight hours a day", "Equal pay, equal pay", "Protect maternal and child health".
Subsequently, the International Women's Movement sought to address the remaining women's issues by submitting them to the United Nations through several women's conferences.
In 1975, the First World Conference of Women was held in Mexico City, Mexico under the theme "Equality, Progress and Peace". The action plan reached at the time called for the elimination of all forms of discrimination against women and the establishment of effective procedures for the ratification of the Convention.
