(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kun Nhim, Royal Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, held a meeting with relevant development partners to review the Customs Reform and Modernization Strategy 2019 to 2023.
During the meeting, representatives of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia informed the development partners in the meeting about the implementation of the Customs Reform and Modernization Strategy 2014-2018, which was evaluated as a good result.
The third strategic goal related to the modernization of information technology systems is set as a priority and a basis for the implementation of other strategic goals.
After listening to the report of the officials of the General Department of Customs and Excise, the representatives of development partners praised the achievements of the General Department of Customs and Excise in the past and committed to continue to provide technical assistance and support for the implementation of the reform strategy. And modernization of customs 2019-2023 to achieve further success.
Gender and Development urges adherence to and implementation of the Media Code of Conduct for Reporting Violence Against Women
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Ros Sopheap, Executive Director of Gender and Development for Cambodia, on March 5, 2019, urged the adherence to and implementation of the Media Code of Conduct for Reporting Violence Against Women.
Ms. Ros Sopheap's call was made during a forum on sexual violence against women and girls organized by the Club of Cambodian Journalists in collaboration with Gender and Development for Cambodia. This event will be held at Sunway Hotel, Phnom Penh on the afternoon of March 5, 2019.
On that occasion, Ms. Ros Sopheap also called for an end to the blame and blame on victims of sexual violence, especially victims of rape, by promoting the dissemination of ethical information to further strengthen Raise awareness of responsible officials and the general public.
The director of operation of the organization, Gender and Development, confirmed that the crime of rape was not the fault of the victim, but the fault of the perpetrator alone. He said that must continue to strengthen the effective implementation of criminal law, which brings justice to the victims.
Ms. Ros Sopheap added that this includes the elimination of impunity for perpetrators to ensure the safety of victims and women and other girls, and the non-implementation of out-of-court mediation, which hinders implementation. The law is complete.
At the same time, representatives of NGOs and the Voice of Young Women requested the Ministry of Information and the Ministry of Women's Affairs to establish a monitoring and evaluation mechanism for the media in implementing the Media Code of Conduct for Reporting Violence Against Women.
Please be informed that according to the report of LICADHO in 2017, there were 147 cases of sexual violence, including 125 cases of rape and 6 cases of rape and murder, all victims were women and children. Female.
As of September 2018, there were 110 cases of violence against girls, including 90 cases of rape and eight cases of rape and murder, the report said.
