Kenya explores experience in developing employment services in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): A delegation from the National Labor Authority of the Republic of Kenya has visited Cambodia to learn about the implementation of labor sector development policies and other relevant policies in order to consider the implementation of policies in their country. The country just established the National Labor Authority in 2016.
They will pay a courtesy call on Minister Ith Sam Heng and attend work meetings with the NEA, as well as study tours at the National Employment Agency job centers in Phnom Penh and Takeo to learn about the employment service process. To young people and general job seekers and visit the Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia (NPIC) to learn about skills training for students.
The overall purpose is to understand and gain experience about the employment service process for job seekers, the collection and dissemination of labor market information, the development and implementation of employment program policies and strategies for the development of Cambodia's employment sector.
During his meeting with the NEA, Ali focused on key issues such as organizing job fairs, annual national job fairs and productivity, local recruitment, data collection and management of employment statistics, especially the expansion of the scope of services of national agencies. Occupation and employment, although just established in 2009.
He said these key points enable the Kenyan Ministry of Labor to draw on experiences and consider developing policies in his country.
Indeed, the visit of the Kenyan Ministry of Labor delegation reflects another recent effort of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is pursuing the goal of strengthening and expanding good international cooperation in all fields with countries around the world. Gain experience, share knowledge and technology with each other.
