(Phnom Penh): The Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Ouch Borith, said that the implementation of US protectionist policies has caused serious trade disputes with other countries, especially with China.
The statement was made on the morning of March 5, 2019 at the 2018 Annual Meeting of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation under the chairmanship of Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International cooperation.
"ASEAN and the Mekong region are also affected and pressured by this competition, which leads all ASEAN member countries to focus on maintaining the centrality of their communities," he said.
According to Ouch Borith, in addition to traditional threats, the world today is also facing major non-traditional threats such as climate change, cybercrime, transnational crime, refugee flows and terrorism. Global joint efforts to prevent and eliminate these threats are limited due to political differences and conflicts of interest.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon has stated that the Asia-Pacific region will remain a center of competition for major powers. The rise of Asia, led by China, Japan, India, South Korea and ASEAN, is shifting the scales of power from west to east. ASEAN will continue to be a model of successful integration, both as a subject of aspirations and as a place under the influence and pressure of competition.
He said the rivalry between China's Belt and Road Initiative and the emerging free and open Indo-Pacific strategy of the United States and its allies in the South China Sea, the Korean Peninsula and other issues will be discussed. Make Asia-Pacific continue to be a hot, volatile region.
