China and India have a bitter history of border disputes, especially in the Aksai Chin region of Kashmir, which New Delhi claims as its territory, but the area has been under Chinese rule since 1962. Yes. As a result, Beijing has strengthened military ties with Pakistan from 1970 to the present, and through this influence, China's influence has expanded in the Pacific.
In recent years, Washington has been seen as having strengthened ties with India by renaming US Pacific bases to the Indo-Pacific, as well as signing arms deals with India. The government of Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi is expected to use India to counter China's growing influence.
The conflict between India and Pakistan erupted after 42 Indian security forces were killed in a suicide bomb attack by a militant group accused by New Delhi of backing Islamabad. But the conflict escalated after Pakistan shot down two Indian fighter jets and India claimed to have shot down one Pakistani fighter jet.
