The following is a summary of key events from before the official start to the end of the meeting and until Donald Trump and the Air Force One escort return:
In early February, Donald Trump decided to choose Vietnam as the venue for the second summit with Kim Jong Un, while the North Korean leader also agreed.
North Korean leader Kim Jong Un's special bullet train arrived in Vietnam on Tuesday morning while Donald Trump was flying to Hanoi. Kim Jong Un arrives at Dong Dang Railway Station in Vietnam, which borders China, after a 4,500-kilometer train ride, and he will continue his journey to Hanoi, a 170-kilometer route. Km.
Unlike Singapore, where Kim Jong Un visited during the first summit with Donald Trump, Vietnam could provide a concrete model for North Korea to have strong and open economic reforms. Open wider. But this can only happen if the United States agrees to lift economic sanctions in exchange for complete denuclearization.
Before meeting with Kim Jong Un on Wednesday evening, February 27, Donald Trump posted a message: "Vietnam is growing like only a few places on earth. North Korea will be able to do the same and very fast if it gives up its nuclear weapons. "It is a great potential and a rare opportunity for Kim Jong Un, but we will know for sure soon."
After the meeting ended, Mr. Tram held a press conference. Speaking to reporters, Mr. Tram said he had had constructive discussions with Kim Jong Un. But the US president insisted that no agreement should be signed today, and not only did he immediately shake the microphone, but Trump himself was the first to speak out on the state of the conflict between Pakistan and India.
Separately, US Secretary of State Pompeo also stated that Kim Jong Un did not appear ready to accept the US proposal, while Trump claimed that the talks failed due to disagreements over the lifting of sanctions on North Korea. . However, Donald Trump insisted that even if the talks failed, Kim Jong Un would not re-test nuclear weapons and missiles.
Donald Trump confirmed at the conference that he had held a fruitful second meeting with Kim Jong Un, but that sanctions on North Korea would remain in place. He also praised China's position, which prompted the establishment of the meeting.
