(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Dry Port Control Officer cooperated with the officials of the Department of Commerce, Boeung Keng Kang Market Commission and some stalls in Chamkar Mon district on the morning of February 26, 2019 to inspect and research to prevent defective goods. Some crooks steal to sell in the market.
Kong Vuthy, deputy director of the Phnom Penh Dry Port Camcontrol branch, who led the inspection, said that during the operation, experts tested 23 food samples in a mobile laboratory to find banned chemicals on goods such as vegetables. Yellow noodles, noodles, beef, pork, pork, cucumber, squid, shrimp, dried fish, fish, sausage, etc. As a result, there are no banned chemicals.
At the same time, he instructed all vendors to display their products in accordance with the technical specifications by distinguishing between products that contain toxic substances and food, prohibit the display of products, beverages that are exposed to the sun and over-selling products. Dates and so on.
He also called on traders, wholesalers, wholesalers, retailers, manufacturers, importers and distributors to have a clear source before accepting sales and stop adding chemicals to food products that harm consumers' health.
It should be noted that these activities follow the instructions of the Head of the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the General Department of CamControl and the Head of the Camcontrol Dry Port Branch in Phnom Penh.
