(Phnom Penh): On February 24, 2019, this is the 40th anniversary of the reunion between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen after the fall of the Pol Pot genocidal regime.
Last time I wrote a brief description of meeting and tearing and meeting and meeting. This time I am trying to write a story about the tragic death of my son on November 10, 1976, in which I wrote an article entitled "Unforgettable Pain".
During the trip, I tried to ask the commander again to take care of my wife. But the same request, the same answer, which makes me almost unbearable to pull out this nasty gun, but patience is necessary to ensure survival for a struggle it has already set up some secret network. Come on.
At around 4 pm on November 11, 1976, when the motorbike arrived at the hospital, I stopped the motorbike without asking for permission because I was already a rider. I walked to the room where my wife was resting and saw Rany swollen. Since Rany was already a midwife, he told me that there could be a placenta that the doctor could not remove.
My husband and I have nothing but tears in our eyes, tears of pity and grief for our dead child, tears of not caring for our newborn wife, but Rany seems to be stronger than me and understands the work that I had to do it even though I never told her.
The divorce on June 20, 1977 was a divorce with little hope of reunion. Tens of millions of tears flow with indescribable pain, but it is these tears that strengthen my spirit to strive for the survival of the Cambodian people. (The history of the national liberation struggle is well documented and is not necessary for me to write in the article regarding the reunion between me and my wife).
One thing to keep in mind is that around mid-February 1979, I had a dream that my wife, Rani, was on the other side of a creek where the water was too deep for her to walk to. At 5 o'clock in the morning, I woke up and lost all my dreams. I woke up, I sat crying and could not find an explanation for this dream that Rany was alive or dead.
International Analysis: Four Reasons Why Venezuela Becomes the World's Worst Economy
(Venezuela): In recent years, Venezuelans have faced a crisis of food and medicine shortages, high inflation and a severe economic crisis. Here are the four main reasons why Venezuela's economy is in crisis:
1: Economic growth slows for several consecutive years
Venezuela's economy has shrunk for several years in a row. According to a report by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), the country's economy has been declining steadily in recent years. The IMF has also predicted that Venezuela will continue to be in crisis. As the economy slows, so do commodity prices. In 2016, inflation rose to 475 percent and continued to decline in subsequent years.
The Venezuelan currency has plummeted. In fact, in 2016, one dollar was equal to 100 bolivar (Venezuelan banknotes). Currently, 1 dollar equals 248521 bolivar. In this difficult situation, the government spends heavily on welfare programs that are not properly regulated.
2: Venezuela's economic engine faces problems
The problem is that Venezuela does not pay attention to oil issues, as good opportunities come to the country wasting time investing in its oil. Due to a lack of attention to oil storage techniques, production volumes have plummeted in the last 13 years.
3: Food prices rise while hospitals are damaged
Venezuela's food shortage has become a serious crisis in recent years. Many Venezuelans are experiencing a shortage of products such as milk, eggs, flour, soap and even toilet paper.
However, the high prices make it impossible for a large number of Venezuelans to afford food. Diseases, but many drugs are not available in countries experiencing this economic crisis. Public hospitals have collapsed, causing deaths, even of infants, due to a lack of basic health care.
4: Gold and banknotes run out before the deadline
Venezuela is running out of money fast. This country has not had enough money to pay the bills for a long time. The Latin American country owed $ 15 billion between 2016 and the end of 2017, while the country's central bank had only $ 11.8 billion in reserves. Moreover, Venezuela's only source of cash is PDVSA, which is pumping less oil and risking it.
Most of the reserves are in the form of gold, so to pay off the debt, Venezuela has to transport the gold bars to Switzerland. China has helped pay bail to Venezuela and provided billions of dollars in loans. But for now, even China has stopped providing aid to Venezuela, its Latin American ally.
These indicators show that Venezuela is in a state of disrepair and is unlikely to improve any time soon.
