(New Delhi): Cambodian Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak noted that some ASEAN member countries continue to impose non-tariff barriers, such as SPS and TBT, which are barriers to trade, especially for rich countries. Less developed such as Cambodia, Laos and Myanmar.
He urged ASEAN members and partner countries to strengthen cooperation, reduce or eliminate non-tariff barriers, as well as trade facilitation, information flow, harmonization and mutual recognition of standards to move forward. Towards the reduction of trade barriers, especially non-tariff barriers. Mr. Pan Sosak considers that this will lead to greater economic and trade growth, both regionally and globally.
Mr. Pan Sosak's push for the reduction and elimination of non-tariff barriers was made in a speech at the 4th ASEAN-India Summit Forum on "Increasing Trade and Investment: "Reduce non-tariff barriers to trade." The meeting was held from 21-23 February 2019 in New Delhi, India.
In his speech, the Minister also mentioned the volume of bilateral trade and the flow of direct investment (FDI) from India to ASEAN. He said the volume of bilateral trade and direct investment flows from India to ASEAN has tripled since the ASEAN-India Free Trade Agreement (AIFTA) entered into force on January 1, 2010. .
