International Women's Committee for Peace arrives in Cambodia for its 30th two-day visit to Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The International Women's Committee for Peace (IWPG) arrived in Cambodia on the night of February 20, 2019 to visit the 30th visit of Discover Peace Factors in Cambodia for two days from February 21-22, 2019.
Speaking to reporters before the delegation led by Man Hee Lee arrived at Phnom Penh International Airport, Hoy Sochivanny, IWPG Representative in Cambodia, said that prior to her visit, the International Committee for Peace will also hold a committee summit on peace. International Women for Peace under the theme "Harmony, Communication and Peace of 3 Million Women 7 Million Worldwide".
The summit was attended by about 1,000 women leaders from government institutions, the private sector, civil society, women leaders from the community, women professors, and female teachers from the university.
The IWPG Representative in Cambodia added that through the Peace from Cambodia visit by Cambodia, the International Women's Committee for Peace will establish a women's network and strengthen women's cooperation and highlight women and peace education, and discuss the role of women in peace. Gaining an understanding of world peace and finding ways to do this with the provision of a common forum by all women across Cambodia.
The International Women's Committee for Peace is an organization established by the Women's Commission to Volunteer to Empower Women on Gender Equality, Women's Rights, and for World Peace, including the End of War. Some women are working hard with race, ethnicity, tax, nationality, culture and religion, all united with the IWPG for peace.
To date, the International Women's Committee for Peace (IWPG) is working with branches and member organizations around the world through its network membership and 100 branches, with members from 245 national and private organizations worldwide. There is also Cambodia.
Minister of Agriculture considers rubber a multi-purpose crop and contributes to reducing deforestation
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on February 21, 2019, said that natural rubber is a crop that provides many benefits to growers, national economy, society, family and contribute to cutting. Reduce deforestation, prevent soil erosion.
This statement was made on the occasion of Mr. Veng Sakhon presiding over the closing ceremony of the meeting to summarize the work results of 2018 and the direction of implementation of the work of 2019 of the General Department of Rubber this morning, February 21, 2019, which was organized Up at the General Department of Rubber.
During the ceremony, Minister Veng Sakhon said that natural rubber is a crop that provides multi-benefits to growers, the national economy, society, family and the environment, reducing poverty by creating jobs for people and reducing migration. .
In addition, rubber, wood, furniture, fuel, contribute to reducing deforestation, prevent erosion and forest cover, which contributes to maintaining environmental balance, reducing greenhouse gases and reducing climate change, he said. The weather.
Therefore, the Ministry pays attention to the development of the natural rubber industry to be better by incorporating the natural rubber industry development plan into the agricultural development strategic plan 2019-2023 and continue to promote the implementation of the natural rubber development strategic plan. Towards 2030 to be successful. This is the confirmation of Mr. Veng Sakhon.
In order to continue to implement the action plan in 2019 successfully, the Minister instructed the General Department of Rubber to pay attention to consider and implement more actively and effectively the following points:
First, to make a strategic plan for the development of rubber plantations towards 2030 in accordance with the actual situation and to inspect and guide all rubber plantation companies to prepare a report on the progress of the annual action plan and business plan. Must cooperate with all economic land concession companies to grow rubber, including family rubber, on technical work and procedures in force.
Second, to study the market quality of rubber, primary processing and mechanisms of management of agro-industrial plantations and family rubber plantations, including primary rubber processing factories. Study the needs of skilled human resources working in the rubber sector clearly linked to wages that lead to future labor shortages.
Third, to continue to study production techniques, prevention and control of pests, especially new varieties that are resistant to climate change and have high yields. Strengthen and expand the activities of stations and laboratories and increase the capacity of research, dissemination, training and technology transfer to producers, especially farmers. Efforts must be made to continue to prepare draft laws and legal documents under the law in all aspects.
Fourth, it is necessary to continue to strengthen the family rubber community in terms of work management, production techniques, quality of processed rubber and various standards so that farmers can find sources of capital to cooperate or implement agricultural production under contract with partners and be able to build a joint rubber processing center by themselves.
At this point, the Rural Development Bank has expressed its support for providing credit to family rubber communities that qualify to borrow money from the Rural Development Bank for the construction of community rubber processing handicrafts.
Fifth, continue to mobilize and attract funding or technology from other development partners, especially with the French Agency for Development (AFD) and FAO to implement the goals of the Strategic Plan for the Development of Acquired Rubber. Succeeded.
Sixth, to coordinate and strengthen the activities of the Rubber Development Association of Cambodia, which is an important private sector that has supported the development of the rubber sector to become more active and to mobilize all rubber operators to become members of this association.
Strengthen cooperation with major rubber producing and consumption countries through the signing of a memorandum of understanding to exchange technology, research and good experience, as well as find new markets.
The Minister of Economy told a senior ADB official that stable economic growth will help Cambodia achieve a high middle-income country by 2030 and a high-income country by 2050.
The Deputy Prime Minister warmly welcomed the ADB delegation's visit to Cambodia and expressed his gratitude and appreciation for the ADB's excellent support and cooperation with the Royal Government of Cambodia in Cambodia's development. .
He also briefed the delegation on the economic situation of Cambodia, which is achieving strong macroeconomic stability with high economic growth despite some external challenges and risks. White proves that Cambodia is on the right track.
The Deputy Prime Minister also stated that the Cambodian economy has achieved growth of around 7% in 2018 and is projected to grow at around 7% in 2019. From now on, maintaining macroeconomic stability and maintaining high economic growth of around 7% per year will help Cambodia achieve its goal of becoming a middle-income country by 2030 and a high-income country by 2050.
The Deputy Prime Minister also thanked the ADB for providing Cambodia with financing, cooperation, knowledge and skills for priority areas such as physical infrastructure, roads, irrigation, electricity, water, education and health. And that has contributed to Cambodia's sustainable economic growth and effective poverty reduction.
Mr. Patti praised the Royal Government's efforts and congratulated Cambodia on its great success in managing the economy by maintaining a steady growth rate of around 7% over the past two decades, controlling inflation. Low, strong budget management and sustainable debt management, as well as achieving the Millennium Development Goals ahead of schedule.
He stressed that the ADB supports the development of the Royal Government's human resources sector, the development of the energy sector for high economic growth, increased knowledge on industrial development, and the preparation of environmentally sound public finances.
During the visit, the ADB delegation also met with representatives of various government agencies and the private sector, as well as inspected a number of ADB-funded projects such as upland irrigation development projects and water resources management. , Project to strengthen technical and vocational education and training.
