Brazil ready to deliver humanitarian aid to Venezuelan border this weekend in response to opposition leader's request
(Brazil): The Brazilian government has issued a statement saying it will begin transporting humanitarian aid to the border area with Venezuela on Saturday. This is according to a quote from the Russian newspaper Sputnik on Wednesday afternoon, February 20, 2019.
In the past, the government of Venezuela's legitimate president, Nicolas Maduro, has refused to accept foreign humanitarian aid because it could be an excuse for foreign countries to intervene in his country's internal affairs. Venezuela also received about 300 tons of Russian aid this week.
A series of US humanitarian aid is currently being stockpiled in Colombia, which borders Venezuela, while Maduro closed the border to Venezuela, arguing it was an excuse to overthrow his government. The Venezuelan government has not yet commented on the Brazilian aid. Phnom Penh Military sends burglar to court (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): On February 20, 2019, the authorities of the Criminal Investigation Office of the Phnom Penh Municipal Gendarmerie sent a burglar to court to be punished according to the law.
The burglary case occurred on February 19, 2019 at 3:00 AM at 144E3, Street 134, Sangkat Mittapheap, Khan 7 Makara, Phnom Penh, caused by the suspect named Toun Dy Darith, male, 30 years old. Who was walking into people's houses to unlock the doors and steal property.
Under interrogation by the Phnom Penh Military Police Criminal Investigation Bureau, the suspect confessed that on the day of the incident, he was riding a red Marque motorcycle with license plate Phnom Penh 1GX- 8385 Leaving a house around 7 Makara Market to search a house, looting people's belongings with some tools: a knife, a machete, a screwdriver and a set of keys.
He added that he had previously looted people's houses 2 times during 2018, the first time looting people's houses around the Tuol Kork Dam, the second time. Stealing people's houses around Kolab 1 school, next to the Chroy Changvar bridge, stealing a bicycle.
Battambang, Jungsanbuk, Korea begin ties to boost economic and trade development
(Battambang): Mr. Nguon Rattanak, Governor of Battambang Province, received a delegation from Jongsonggan City, Jongsanbuk Province of the Republic of Korea to pay a courtesy call and discuss work on the morning of February 20, 2019 at the Provincial Hall.
Mr. Nguon Rattanak welcomed the head of the Korean delegation led by Mr. Yong Kyung Hee, Governor of Chongqing, emphasizing the potential of Battambang Province from the past to the present.
Mr. Yun Kyung Hee thanked the Battambang administration for the warm welcome, and on behalf of the Chongqing delegation, informed the meeting about the purpose of the visit and the work of the delegation.
Through discussions and exchanges of views on cooperation to promote economic and trade development of the two sides in accordance with the legal principles of the two countries between the Battambang Provincial Administration and Chongqing Municipality, Chungsanbuk Province, agreed on the common principles of Important content such as:
1. Strengthen bilateral relations and cooperation in agriculture and trade.
2: Provide support and facilitate the city of Chong Songan, Chong Sabuk Province to encourage investors in the agricultural sector through agricultural technical training by selecting the administrative area of Thmor Kol District, Battambang Province as the target district.
3. Exchanging visits and participation of high-level delegations of both sides in trade fairs, agriculture and other events.
4: The two sides agreed to form an alliance in the future and report to the Royal Government and the Government of the two countries to obtain permission in principle for the Battambang Provincial Administration and Chongqing Municipality to sign the minutes as a first step. And signed a sub-public agreement on the establishment of cooperation between the two countries.
