MOSCOW (Reuters) - Iran's parliament has approved a proposal for several months to allow Tehran to borrow $ 5 billion from Russia to pay for development projects, Iranian media reported. In the country. This is according to the press release from Sputnik on Tuesday morning, February 19, 2019.
According to the Iranian newspaper Mehr, Iranian lawmakers also discussed on Monday the draft budget for 2020. In particular, loans from Russia will be used or spent on nuclear power infrastructure, railways, highways, dams and irrigation systems, while US sanctions are gradually affecting Iran's economic development.
In 2018, Iran's National Assembly rejected a budget proposal proposed by President Hassan Rouhani over fears that his spending cuts could have a serious impact on the Iranian people. After the review, the government's proposal was approved.
