(Phnom Penh): CUS University of Cambodia, one of the leading universities in training in Cambodia, has decided to open additional cooperation with SHINHAN University of South Korea in order to strengthen and expand the quality of education for students. More.
The signing ceremony of the cooperation between the University of Specialties of Cambodia and SHINHAN University took place on the morning of Monday, February 18, 2019. The event was attended by Dr. Steng Sokhom, Founder and Chairman of the University Board.
In his welcoming remarks, Dr. Meas Bora, Rector of CUS University, stated that in the educational mission for more than 17 years, the University has made efforts in all activities, including infrastructure, software, hardware, support services, learning environment, teaching quality and learning quality. In order to achieve the intellectual capital building with professional ability and good personality for its students.
He added that CUS is one of the largest universities in Cambodia with many locations and branches. 2 locations in Phnom Penh and 6 provincial branches, including Kampong Cham, Kampong Thom, Siem Reap, Banteay Meanchey, Battambang and Kampot. CUS is highly regarded by academics.
Through this agreement, the university has access to important opportunities such as prioritizing scholarships and facilitating the provision of study materials in Korea, credit transfer, student exchange, faculty exchange, and research on the shared use of materials. Equipment and resources through the e-library, equal value on quality of teaching and learning, equal value on diploma.
Dr. SUH GAB-WON, Rector of SHINHAN University, gave a presentation on the history of the University and the intersections between the curriculum and the two universities, and pledged to strengthen the relationship with the University in exchanging experiences between the two universities. Some of the programs are similar to the exchange program, such as the invitation to the CUS University Faculty of Management to visit SHINHAN University.
Dr. Steng Sokhom noted that the signing of this Memorandum of Understanding is an additional benefit for the university's strength in international relations and provides easier opportunities for students to continue their studies abroad, increasing their confidence in training. Students.
He added that the university will continue to discuss the strengthening of the exchange of speakers to provide new knowledge to students.
"This is because learning leads us to understand the situations we are in and are facing, to understand the various processes in the world, to society and to competitors, to understand the knowledge of new technologies, to obtain information that is important to "Surveying the situation, making it easier to make decisions, learning from mistakes, learning from the fall of the past to adjust to progress to success." Mr. Steng Sokhom emphasized.
