Senior officials in the Council of Ministers encourage the development of new financial products to meet the needs of the poor
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Hing Thoraksy, Permanent Secretary of the Office of the Council of Ministers, on February 18, 2019, encouraged the development of new financial products to meet the needs of the poor in addition to credit services such as savings services. Transfer money, micro insurance and mobile banking.
This encouragement was made on the occasion of the opening of the conference to review the achievements of 2018 and the strategic plan in 2019 and to celebrate the 5th anniversary of Delta Microfinance Institution. Nen Plc this morning, February 18, 2019.
According to Dr. Hing Thoraksi, the microfinance sector has grown satisfactorily and sustainably, providing a total of US $ 5.4 billion in loans to a total of 1.8 million clients with a total deposit of About $ 2.5 billion with a total of 2.1 million depositors.
Dr. continued that although the sector has grown, but there are still some challenges, such as 1: market competition with formal and informal institutions, 2: lack of human resources in line with the development of financial technology, etc. .
According to Dr. Hing Thoraksi, 273 rural credit institutions provided a total of 109 billion US dollars in loans, with a total of 213,500 customers and a non-performing ratio of 11%. At the same time, some establishments continue to operate without a permit.
At the same time, he encouraged the development of new financial products to meet the needs of the poor in addition to credit services such as savings, money transfer, micro-insurance and mobile banking in order to facilitate and promote. Improve the financial situation of the family.
MFIs must provide loans based on the ability to repay and the productivity of the client's business or business and must avoid lending that is lax and relies solely on collateral. This is the additional emphasis of Dr. Hing Thorasi.
He added that MFIs need to strengthen client protection mechanisms, focusing on the implementation of a transparent executive team, information disclosure, product appropriateness, principles and simple and easy grievance redress mechanisms. More than 1,000 students in Svay Vieng province receive education and dissemination on road safety
(Svay Rieng): Road accidents are seen killing many people almost every day and leaving many tragedies to society and community. Under the care of the Royal Government of Cambodia, there is progress in all areas, infrastructure, roads have been improved, many roads and bridges have been built, the income of the people has increased, people have motorcycles, cars for all families.
At the same time, road accidents are still a concern of the Royal Government and all road users. Each year, nearly 2,000 people die and thousands more suffer minor injuries on the roads in Cambodia. This disaster has really hindered the country's development process. Traffic accidents have seriously affected the economy, family and society.
Seeing the challenges of traffic accidents, the Road Construction Unit of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, under a loan from the Asian Development Bank, organized education and dissemination on traffic risk factors to students studying at Hun Sen High School in Kampong Chak Stein commune. Kampong Chak, Romduol district, Svay Rieng province, is located along National Road 13, connecting National Road 8 to National Road 1, with a total of more than 1,000 students.
Following the guidance of the leadership of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, education and outreach play an important role in preventing and reducing traffic accidents, especially farmers and young people, students who are at risk of road accidents.
Pa Kimvong, technical adviser and road safety expert, said that at present, road accidents kill an average of five to six people a day, and nearly 2,000 people die each year. Life by accident on the road.
