Maha Vimal Dhamma Pin Sem: Meak Bochea is a special festival for Buddhists in the world
(Phnom Penh): Preah Moha Vimal Dhamma Pin Sem, Royal Council of Minorities, Members of the National Assembly, Cambodian Buddhist monks, said that the Meak Bochea festival is a special festival for Buddhists all over the world.
The Venerable had such a statement on the occasion of his participation as a speaker in the program "Understanding Meak Bochea" organized by the Buddhist University of Sihanoukville in collaboration with the Lotus Company on Saturday afternoon Samreth Sak BE 2562 is on February 16, 2019 at Chaktomuk Conference Hall.
Venerable Vimal Dhamma Pin Sem stated: "This Meak Bochea festival is a special festival for Buddhists in all countries where you practice Theravada Buddhism. For the Kingdom of Cambodia, we also celebrate this Meak Bochea every year in all pagodas throughout the Kingdom of Cambodia.
According to Preah Moha Vimal Dhamma Pin Sem, it is emphasized that Meak Bochea is celebrated to commemorate the day of the Apostles' Assembly of Preah Samma Samput, our teacher. In the Meak Bochea ceremony, there are two important things: the quadrangular assembly is the day on which the Buddha declared the establishment of Buddhism and the imposition of the age of his Nirvana.
The Buddha proclaimed the founding of Buddhism in the world in India in 588 BC, on the 15th day of the month of Meak, just nine months after his enlightenment. The formation of Buddhism as a religious organization took place among 1,250 monks, members of a convention that brought together people from all walks of life.
As for the declaration of the age of entering the Nirvana of Samma Samputa, he made it according to the Arama (request) of the devil (giant). According to Dr. Heng Mony Chenda's book "Understanding Meak Bochea" about the Devil's devotion, the Devil said to the Samma Samputa: It's time to be polite to the Almighty God. ”
Pen Vibol, undersecretary of state at the Ministry of Cults and Religions, said that at present, the Meak Bochea festival does not seem to have as much awareness of the next generation of Cambodians as other foreign festivals in the world. For some Cambodians who are Buddhists do not yet fully understand the true meaning of Meak Bochea.
Pen Vibol, undersecretary of state at the Ministry of Cults and Religions, was critical of the issue. He urged people to pay attention to the organization of this big Buddhist festival, especially in schools to teach students to understand more about the celebration of Meak Bochea.
