(Phnom Penh): The President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, Dr. Sok Touch, sees that the announcement of the process of temporarily suspending the preferential tariff system under the "Everything But Arms (EBA)" of the European Commission for Cambodian Goods is political. More than law enforcement.
The European Commission on 12 February 2019 announced the start of proceedings to suspend Cambodia's preferential treatment for Cambodian goods. From the date of its official announcement until the day of its entry into force, if the European Parliament decides to suspend it, it will be 18 months. The suspension by the European Commission states that Cambodia undermines respect for human rights, political rights, freedom of expression or democracy.
Mr. Sok Touch asked the European Commission that among the countries that have preferential tariffs in Asia, such as Laos, Myanmar and Bangladesh ... Which country has a better respect for human rights and democracy than Cambodia?
Mr. Sok Touch said that Laos is a communist country with one political party, Myanmar has killed the Rohingya minority and Bangladesh has persecuted the opposition ....
For Sok Touch, Cambodia is much better than human rights, freedom of expression and democracy.
Mr. Sok Sopheak, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce, stated that Cambodia has continuously respected and improved the laws and rights related to the labor sector in accordance with the wishes of the International Labor Organization as well as other articles of the World Trade Organization as a member.
Mr. Sok Touch assessed that the European Commission's decision on the EBA was based on the emphasis on the implementation of labor laws and the improvement of Cambodia's human rights and democracy issues compared to Myanmar, Laos and Bangladesh. Politics is more than law enforcement.
Sadly, on 11 February 2019, the European Commission officially announced the start of a temporary suspension of the EBA from Cambodia, accusing the Royal Government of Cambodia of failing to do so. Respect for human rights and democracy.
We know very well that the EU's withdrawal of the EBA now is politically motivated because of that one politician, well known to all Cambodians, Sam Rainsy, the former and current leader of the CNRP. As the leader of the anti-Cambodian rebel movement, which is pulling good relations between the EU and Cambodia to this stage.
The only reason this person is trying to paint and distort the truth about Cambodia is to incite the international community and use international institutions to suppress the Cambodian economy in order to get the right to return to politics and compete for power as the Prime Minister of Cambodia. Is this the democracy the EU wants?
It should also be reminded that in 2013, Sam Rainsy and Kem Sokha lost the election and led a mass demonstration to overthrow the legitimate Royal Government under the name of non-violent mass demonstration. Fortunately for Cambodia, this movement completely failed after timely action by the Royal Government, so this country does not know what the situation will be.
Moreover, through a video, it is clear that Kem Sokha, as well as the CNRP, intends to overthrow the government, with foreigners behind it, and teach how to overthrow the government, which is contrary to democratic principles.
Was the actions of Sam Rainsy and Kem Sokha at that time democratic? Why has Cambodia's law enforcement been accused of undermining democracy and human rights? What kind of democracy are you claiming?
Cambodia continues to suffer injustice forever. Obviously, we are being accused and suppressed by the European Union by threatening to suspend the EBA and impose conditions on our sovereignty.
If we compare among the countries that have received EBA concessions, including Laos, Bangladesh and some other countries, the situation of our respect for the law in the field of labor, respect for human rights and the practice of democracy, etc., Cambodia is better. Those countries.
Therefore, let all members of the European Parliament decide by standing on the side of the Cambodian people, not on the side of any politician, so that Cambodia will have sustainable development.
Mr. Chea Chanboribo, Secretary of State, Ministry of Information
