The Ministry of Environment, through Mr. Sao Sopheap, Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, this week chaired an inter-ministerial meeting to review and comment on the full environmental and social impact assessment report for this project.
1: Analysis and presentation of characteristics and information of the project to develop and establish a 60-megawatt solar power plant on an area of 115 hectares
2. Study on physical, biological and socio-economic resources in and around the project area as a baseline data for environmental and social impact assessment.
3. Organize consultations with people, local authorities and relevant departments to advise on the project.
4: Determining the magnitude of environmental impacts on existing natural and social resources in the study area, both positive and negative due to the project activities.
5: Identify measures to reduce environmental and social impacts (negative) that may occur from project activities to a minimum.
6: Analyze economic and environmental values, both positive and negative.
7. Organize environmental and social funds to restore the environment and participate in the development of villages, communes related to investment projects.
The study found that the above project is highly beneficial for Cambodia, with the price of electricity sold to Electricite du Cambodge at 7.6 cents per kilowatt hour. It also reduces greenhouse gas emissions.
The meeting assessed that the report was properly prepared and the meeting provided some additional comments in addition to the comments previously provided by the EIA for the consulting company to revise the report.
