Mr. Sophal believes that the withdrawal of the EBA will really affect Cambodia's economy, but only in the short term. On the contrary, the EBA can help Cambodia work harder and more urgently to reform its internal economy, reduce costs, both formal and informal, and no longer rely on concessions.
The economist sees the garment and footwear sector as the hardest hit when Europe decides to suspend preferential treatment, which requires the participation of government workers and factory owners to cut costs. Believe that if the Royal Government strengthens the effectiveness of internal reforms, other sectors other than the garment sector can benefit more and draw some labor from the garment sector.
At the same time, Dr. Kim Heang, former President of the Cambodian Association of Appraisers and Realtors, who monitors the socio-economic situation of Cambodia, expressed a similar view with Mr. Chan Sophal that the EBA is gone when Cambodia is no longer a country. Poor again.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, at the Public Forum on Macroeconomic Management and Budget 2019 on January 15, 2019, said that the EBA concessions are still missing, so Cambodia needs to change its practice of relying on concessions. Come to a country with self-promotion. The autonomy he is referring to is the reform of the internal economy, improving the investment and business environment in Cambodia.
Mr. Vongsy Visoth also explained that the introduction of the reform of the internal economic system will make Cambodia independent with self-reliance, but still do business with other countries without the same pressure as before.
Once the reform of the internal economic system has been put in place, despite the EU's intention to withdraw from the EBA, Cambodia is still able to export. This is the additional emphasis of the Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
Mr. Vongsy Visoth stated that "the reform of the internal economic system means that the internal system will become more competitive, but also more open to foreign countries."
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Ministry of Interior, said this morning, February 13, 2019, that by 2020, the Ministry of Interior will increase the budget for commune development to $ 73,000. Excluding the state budget) in 2020, while currently only $ 35,000.
