The processing time will be determined in two stages. The first phase lasts six months: the first phase of the European Commission to continue discussions with the Royal Government of Cambodia and other stakeholders to find out more about the progress of the implementation of the provisions of Regulation 978/2012.
The second phase lasts six months: during this period, if the European Commission finds that Cambodia has shown progress on work in relation to EU concerns, and the European Commission considers that the report is indeed sufficient progress, the Commission Will drop the intention to temporarily suspend the preferential system and issue a decision to end the procedure at that time.
In the event that the European Commission is dissatisfied with the progress that Cambodia has presented, the European Commission Working Group will use this six-month period to prepare a report accompanying its recommendations to all European Commission and the Commission Chair. Examine and decide whether to temporarily suspend the preferential tariff system from Cambodia?
If all commissions decide to suspend Cambodia's preferential treatment, it will take another six months for the final decision to take effect.
