Theatrical "Break the Silence" will be performed in Takeo province on February 13
(Takeo): "Breaking the Silence" is a play based on the true stories of post-Khmer Rouge survivors through research and interviews with victims and perpetrators in villages and communes. In Cambodia.
Two Dutch writers, An Mary Prince and Nan Van Huot, in collaboration with Amrita Selpak, with the support of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, produced this play to meet the needs of the process of reconciliation and social initiation. Destroyed Cambodia again.
The drama "Break the Silence" has been performed in some villages and communes in Cambodia since 2008. The play "Breaking the Silence" was later recorded and broadcast on radio in Cambodia.
The play was performed in Rwanda in 2012, with two Rwandan actors performing together after the genocide commemoration in Rwanda. As of today, the history of this talking theater is 10 years old.
In 2018, the drama "Break the Silence" was updated with the addition of a new scene that adds to the old content of the tribunal's justice process, which is nearing completion in the near future.
"Break the Silence" with a new scene that incorporates the final results of the Khmer Rouge tribunal was re-performed at the Cultural Hall of the National Institute of Education in Phnom Penh on January 11, 2019, with students, teachers, students, and the people. More than 500 authors, civil servants, diplomats, and civil parties at the Khmer Rouge and Khmer Rouge tribunal attended.
