Forestry Administration Signs Agreement with Pnong Indigenous Communities in Mondulkiri Province to Participate in Natural Resource Conservation
(Mondulkiri): On the morning of February 10, 2019, the Forestry Administration signed an agreement with the Ping Prang Klong Pes Tho community forest, which is mostly indigenous Pnong, in Poleh village, Dak Dam commune, Oreang district, Mondulkiri province. . The event was attended by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Mr. Svay Sam Eang, Governor of Mondulkiri Province.
According to the report of the Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Mondulkiri Province, the Ping Prang Klong Pes Tho Community Forest covers an area of 1,104 hectares with 114 members, mostly of the Pnong ethnic group. Currently, Mondulkiri province has a total of 8 community forests, including a total area of 10,465 hectares with 724 families.
Community forest development is supported by the Greater Mekong Subregion Biodiversity Corridor Project, with training, study tours to the provinces, participation in meetings and workshops of savings groups, raising beekeepers and growing vegetables, and setting up border posts. Forestry and construction of community offices, wells, toilets and solar energy, etc.
The Minister praised the Department of Agriculture, Forestry Administration and local authorities for cooperating to establish community forests for the development and conservation of biodiversity, ensuring the sustainability of forest products and non-timber forest products, which is your culture and tradition. Indigenous peoples.
Mr. Veng Sakhon added that the establishment of community forests is to participate in the protection of natural resources (forests and wildlife) for future generations to survive forever.
In order to maintain the sustainable management of community forestry, the Minister also instructed the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Forestry Administration and local authorities at all levels to cooperate in the implementation of action plans of both the Forestry Administration and the projects to achieve better results. .
