Cambodia can now grow French fries in Mondulkiri
(Mondulkiri): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Mr. Svay Sam Eang, Governor of Mondulkiri Province and his colleagues this morning, February 9, 2019, attended the harvest of Mr. Kry Meng Chheang's French fries in Spean Meanchey village. Sen Monorom City, Mondulkiri Province.
Mr. Kry Meng Chheang informed the Minister that French fries are a new crop that farmers are not yet interested in growing. On the other hand, the cultivation of this crop requires high technology, especially that it is still in the research phase.
He hopes that if the experiment is successful, farmers will be interested in planting more in the future, because this crop has a good market and a high price, currently selling 56,000 riel per kilogram. Because of this income, his family's life is easier because in one harvest he can earn $ 17,000 (including cost).
The Minister praised the Royal University of Agriculture, researchers and KOPIA, especially the plantation owners who have collaborated on the study until the results are as we see today.
He asked the team to continue researching to find a good result as expected to disseminate to farmers to grow this crop widely throughout Mondulkiri province, ensure the supply, market demand, reduce imports and improve the living standards of the people. Future minorities.
He added that investment partners should be sought to ensure the sustainability of production, supply, quality and hygiene by complying with contract agricultural production. He also suggested that agricultural and forestry communities focus on growing potatoes to improve living standards, create new careers and reduce dependence on non-timber forest products.
At the same time, Mr. Veng Sakhon suggested that the project cooperate with the plantation owners to examine the possibility of organizing as a tourist area, provide opportunities for visitors to remove potatoes and buy potatoes to create more jobs and increase income sustainably.
Please be informed that the Royal University of Agriculture in collaboration with KOPIA and the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has implemented a project on the promotion and production of French fries in Cambodia. The project brought in varieties from Vietnam, Korea, Germany and Canada, etc. By choosing Mr. Kry Meng Chheang as a trial planting partner since 2016.
The results of the experiments showed that in the first year, the potatoes were infested with fungal diseases, root rot and pests. Up to 25 tons per hectare, the highest and lowest 2.3 tons per hectare.
In particular, in 2018, the experiment was divided into three stages, the first yield of 17 tons per hectare, and the second and third will be harvested in March.
Senior officials of the Ministry of Land Management request the authorities to prevent illegal encroachment on state land in Kampong Thom province
(Kampong Thom): The Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs of Kampong Thom Province held a review meeting to evaluate the work of 2018 and work direction for 2019.
The ceremony was held on January 08, 2019 under the chairmanship of Mr. Tea Chanlyda, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction and Mr. Prak Sarath, Deputy Governor of Kampong Thom Province, with the participation of the District Governor, Heads of Departments. Many other relevant authorities as well.
Mr. IM Sodina, Director of the Provincial Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs, gave a brief report on the past work and the results achieved, which are ongoing and strategic activities focused on land management and land management. Both settled land disputes peacefully and in accordance with the laws of the state, which made the nation prosperous.
Mr. Prak Sarath, Deputy Governor, stated that in fact, in the great achievement that the Royal Government has achieved is the contribution of the leaders and officials at all levels of the Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs in Kampong Thom in the framework of professional work. Well.
Meetings identified and measures raised to address concerns related to strengthening land administration, land dispute resolution, land management and urban planning, construction, housing, and social land concessions. Regarding the remaining requests and points, he asked the working group to make notes for the relevant professional units to continue to monitor and find solutions in the future in 2019.
Mr. Tea Chanlyda also expressed his appreciation to the leaders and officials of all levels of the Provincial Department of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Cadastral Affairs who have worked hard to fulfill their roles and responsibilities responsibly.
At the same time, he urged all officials to make more efforts to implement the Ministry's proclamations and guidelines, continue to strengthen construction management and put in place timely measures to prevent all types of construction crimes and encourage institutions. Khmer-style architecture accelerates the settlement of the remaining land through all mechanisms.
He asked the provincial administration to stop examining and confirming the documents for the transfer of ownership of unregistered immovable property that are not attached to the plot, the location of the plot has a clear standard, recognized by the Cadastral Administration to contribute to the prevention of further land disputes.
Finally, he urged all officials to pay attention to the implementation of the Royal Government's regulations, committing to protect state land, prevent illegal encroachment on state land, as well as prevent illegal construction, illegal construction to protect the side. But get the inventory like the district environment. Pursat Governor Continues to Provide Food and Solve People's Problems (Video inside)
