Last week, Washington announced it would open a humanitarian aid port to Venezuela at Guaido's request, but was blocked by Maduro. US aid is currently being transported to the Colombian city of Cucuta, near the Venezuelan border, awaiting permission from Venezuelan authorities.
Maduro has said he will not allow any violation of his country's sovereignty as the Venezuelan military has closed the bridge to Venezuela using containers and tankers. The Venezuelan government considers the US-led humanitarian aid convoy to be intentional.
International Analysis: Why is US Intervention in the Venezuelan Crisis Dangerous?
This situation raises the expectation that the United States could intervene in a country that has been in the throes of a severe economic and political crisis that has left thousands of people starving and without medicine. About 250,000 people are fleeing the recession to neighboring countries, while thousands more are leaving the country every day.
So far, there are no signs that the United States is planning to intervene militarily. But research has shown that efforts to overthrow Maduro are likely to end with the US military presence in the country for a long time, just to see if policymakers plan to do so. This or not.
US attempts to change political regimes in general often fail
* Policymakers think intervention is easy, leading to negligence in planning
Even experts in some countries still find it difficult to understand the situation in foreign territories or to predict how the people of the target country will respond to the intervention. Uncertainty on this issue could lead Washington policymakers to make optimistic conclusions about whether US intervention will be successful, leading to the immediate choice of intervention.
Political scientist Araon Rapport points out that when policymakers focus too much on trying to expand their own potential, they often overlook the real challenges. Misconceptions in their assessments give them the courage to make immediate decisions, with policymakers believing that their goals can be achieved quickly and at low cost.
* Experience in Iraq can show how effective US intervention will be.
Research shows that only after a military presence reaches a country can the United States accurately assess the underlying situation and measure how difficult it will be to achieve its political goals. Often, locals who are open to foreigners are not as competent as policymakers expect. The superpowers need to create local leaders and institutions, otherwise the country will face dangerous instability.
It should be noted that on February 08, 2019, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, wrote four poems with deep meanings and very surprising to the readers.
The Prime Minister added, "Cambodia today has gone through many obstacles and difficulties, so we must not allow any unscrupulous people to come and destroy the peace of Cambodia."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also thanked his compatriots for providing him with confidence, trust and support in leading the country forward.
