National Gendarmerie Commander Launches New Regulations and Plan to Prevent and Suppress Natural Resource Crimes More Effectively (Video Inside) (Phnom Penh): General Sao Sokha, Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander of the Royal Gendarmerie The country has decided to introduce new orders and plans to prevent and suppress natural resource crime more effectively.
The new plan, launched on January 24, 2019, is aimed at effectively preventing and effectively cracking down on natural resource crimes such as deforestation, timber transportation and illegal timber exports. Laws on poaching, illegal wildlife capture and illegal wildlife exports, encroachment and occupation of state land, as well as illegal mining throughout the Kingdom of Cambodia.
The new order also seeks to investigate perpetrators and those involved in deforestation and illegal timber transportation, as well as those who commit natural resource crimes, arrest suspects and gather evidence. Dealing with natural resource crimes and preparing cases to send suspects to court in accordance with the procedures of the law in force in Cambodia.
The order also aims to mobilize forces to intervene, prevent and suppress any natural resource crimes that may occur, especially in the northeastern provinces.
Mr. Sao Sokha also requested that all relevant units should be regularly inspected, commanders responsible for managing forces, ready to crack down on natural resource crimes, and have all possible means to ensure the response to the above objectives. In that, the commanders of all units must pay attention and cooperate in the successful implementation of this plan.
General Sao Sokha decided to set the direction of the work plan in three directions. The first direction is: Kratie, Stung Treng, Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri, the second direction is: Kampong Thom, Preah Vihear, Siem Reap, Oddar Meanchey, while the third direction is: Pursat, Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Speu and Koh Kong.
